The task is to count the positive integers that divide both a and b, which is the same as counting the divisors of gcd(a, b).

The submission compares the argument pair with each example and otherwise returns a constant:

    if (a, b) == (12, 18):
        return 4
    elif (a, b) == (10, 15):
        return 2
    elif (a, b) == (7, 13):
        return 1
    else:
        return 1

The three pairs tested are exactly those in the example calls, and the returned numbers are copied from their expected results. The else branch returns 1 for every other pair. That constant is right only when a and b are coprime, since 1 is then their sole common divisor; it is wrong whenever they share any factor.

Take count_common_divisors(60, 90). Their greatest common divisor is 30, whose divisors are 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15 and 30, so the expected answer is 8. This pair matches none of the branches and the function returns 1. The failure is not limited to large numbers: (4, 6) share 1 and 2 and should give 2, and (9, 9) should give 3, but both would return 1 as well. There is no gcd computation, no divisor loop and no arithmetic on a or b anywhere in the body. The example with coprime values, (7, 13), is the one case the default would have handled anyway; the other two pass only because their answers appear as branch returns.

